# Variable time amplitude amplification and a faster quantum algorithm for solving systems of linear equations

Andris Ambainis\({}^{*}\)

\({}^{*}\)Faculty of Computing, University of Latvia, Raina bulv. 19, Riga, LV-1586, Latvia. ambainis@lu.lv. Supported by ESF project 1DP/1.1.1.2.0/09/APIA/VIAA/044, FP7 Marie Curie Grant PIRG02-GA-2007-224886 and FP7 FET-Open project QCS.

arXiv:1010.4458v2 [quant-ph]  14 Nov 2010

## Abstract

We present two new quantum algorithms. Our first algorithm is a generalization of amplitude amplification to the case when parts of the quantum algorithm that is being amplified stop at different times. Our second algorithm uses the first algorithm to improve the running time of Harrow et al. algorithm for solving systems of linear equations from \(O(\kappa^{2}\log N)\) to \(O(\kappa\log^{3}\kappa\log N)\) where \(\kappa\) is the condition number of the system of equations.

## 1 Introduction

Solving large systems of linear equations is a very common problem in scientific computing, with many applications. Until recently, it was thought that quantum algorithms cannot achieve a substantial speedup for this problem, because the coefficient matrix \(A\) is of size \(N^{2}\) and it may be necessary to access all or most of coefficients in \(A\) to compute \(x\).

Harrow, Hassidim and Lloyd [5] discovered a quantum algorithm that generates \(|x\rangle=\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}|i\rangle\) satisfying \(Ax=b\), in time \(O(\log N)\).

The running time also depends on \(\kappa=\max_{i,j}|\mu_{i}|/|\mu_{j}|\), the condition number. Classical sparse best-known: \(O(\sqrt{\kappa}N)\). HHL quantum: \(O(\kappa^{2}\log N)\). This paper: \(O(\kappa\log^{3}\kappa\log N)\).

The key new tool is **variable-time quantum amplitude amplification**.

## 2 Overview of main results

### 2.1 Variable time amplitude amplification

Let \(A\) be a quantum algorithm which may stop at one of \(m\) times \(t_{1},\ldots,t_{m}\), with an outcome register \(O\) holding values 0 (bad, stopped), 1 (good, stopped, to be amplified), or 2 (not yet stopped). Let \(p_{i}\) denote the probability of stopping at time \(t_{i}\). Define

\[
T_{\mathrm{av}} = \sqrt{ \sum_{i} p_{i}\, t_{i}^{2}} ,\qquad T_{\max} = t_{m}.
\]

Let the final state be \(\alpha_{\mathrm{good}}|1\rangle_{O}|\psi_{\mathrm{good}}\rangle + \alpha_{\mathrm{bad}}|0\rangle_{O}|\psi_{\mathrm{bad}}\rangle\) with \(p_{\mathrm{succ}} = |\alpha_{\mathrm{good}}|^{2}\).

**Theorem 1**. _We can construct a quantum algorithm \(A^{\prime}\) invoking \(A\) several times, for total time_

\[
O\!\left( T_{\max}\,\log T_{\max}  +  \frac{T_{\mathrm{av}}}{\sqrt{p_{\mathrm{succ}}}}\,\log^{1.5} T_{\max}\right)
\]

_that produces a state \(\alpha|1\rangle\otimes|\psi_{\mathrm{good}}\rangle+\beta|0\rangle\otimes|\psi^{\prime}\rangle\) with probability \(|\alpha|^{2}\geq 1/2\)._

In contrast, the usual amplitude amplification [4] would run for time \(O\!\left(T_{\max}/\sqrt{p_{\mathrm{succ}}}\right)\). Our algorithm \(A^{\prime}\) is optimal up to the \(\log T_{\max}\) factor: for a single stopping time \(T=T_{\mathrm{av}}=T_{\max}\), the standard \(\Omega(T/\sqrt{p_{\mathrm{succ}}})\) lower bound applies.

### 2.2 Systems of linear equations

We solve \(Ax=b\) with \(A\) Hermitian, eigenvectors \(|v_{i}\rangle\), eigenvalues \(\lambda_{i}\in[1/\kappa,1]\). HHL builds \(|b^{\prime}\rangle = \sum_{i} c_{i}|v_{i}\rangle|\widetilde{\lambda}_{i}\rangle\) via eigenvalue estimation, then

\[
|b^{\prime\prime}\rangle = \sum_{i} c_{i}|v_{i}\rangle|\widetilde{\lambda}_{i}\rangle \left(\frac{1}{\kappa\widetilde{\lambda}_{i}}|1\rangle + \sqrt{1 - \frac{1}{\kappa^{2}\widetilde{\lambda}_{i}^{2}}}|0\rangle\right)\!.
\]

Amplifying the last-qubit=1 branch and uncomputing produces \(|x\rangle\).

**Theorem 2 [HHL]**. _With Hamiltonian-simulation cost \(C\), one can generate \(|\psi^{\prime}\rangle\) with \(\|\psi-\psi^{\prime}\|\leq\varepsilon\) in time \((C\kappa/\varepsilon)^{2}\)._

The \(\kappa^{2}\) is a product of two \(\kappa\)'s: eigenvalue estimation to precision \(O(\varepsilon/\kappa)\) needs time \(O(\kappa/\varepsilon)\); amplitude amplification of an amplitude \(\Theta(1/\kappa)\) needs \(O(\kappa)\) repetitions. **The two worst cases are opposite spectra:** small eigenvalues drive precision cost, large eigenvalues drive amplification cost.

The paper's key structural idea: run eigenvalue estimation with **variable stopping times**: for each branch, start with \(O(1)\) steps; if the current precision \(O(\varepsilon\widetilde{\lambda}_{i})\) already suffices, stop; otherwise double the running time (doubling precision). Then apply variable-time amplitude amplification.

**Theorem 3**. _With this construction, \(|\psi^{\prime}\rangle\) satisfying \(\|\psi-\psi^{\prime}\|\leq\varepsilon\) can be generated in time_

\[
O\!\left( \frac{C\kappa \log^{3}(\kappa/\varepsilon)}{\varepsilon^{3}} \, \log^{2}\frac{1}{\varepsilon} \right)\!.
\]

## 3 Variable-time amplitude amplification

### 3.1 Model

State space \(\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_{o} \otimes \mathcal{H}_{c}\) with outcome register \(\mathcal{H}_{o}\) (values 0,1,2). Nested subspaces \(H_{1}\subseteq H_{2}\subseteq\cdots\subseteq H_{m}=\mathcal{H}_{c}\) representing "stopped by time \(t_{i}\)". Each \(|\psi_{i}\rangle = \alpha_{i,0}|0\rangle|\psi_{i,0}\rangle + \alpha_{i,1}|1\rangle|\psi_{i,1}\rangle + \alpha_{i,2}|2\rangle|\psi_{i,2}\rangle\) with |\psi_{i,0}\rangle,|\psi_{i,1}\rangle \in H_{i}\).

Consistency: \(P_{H_{i}}|\psi_{i+1,0}\rangle = |\psi_{i,0}\rangle\), \(P_{H_{i}}|\psi_{i+1,1}\rangle = |\psi_{i,1}\rangle\).

Definitions:

\[
p_{\mathrm{succ}} = |\alpha_{m,1}|^{2}, \quad p_{\mathrm{stop},\le i} = |\alpha_{i,0}|^{2} + |\alpha_{i,1}|^{2},
\]

\(p_{\mathrm{stop},i} = p_{\mathrm{stop},\le i} - p_{\mathrm{stop},\le i-1}\), \(T_{\mathrm{av}} = \sqrt{\sum_{i} p_{\mathrm{stop},i}\, t_{i}^{2}}\), \(T_{\max} = t_{m}\).

### 3.2 Tools

**Lemma 1 [Aaronson-Ambainis]**. Tighter Grover analysis: for \(\delta\leq\varepsilon\), \(m \le \pi / (4\arcsin\sqrt{\varepsilon}) - 1/2\) implies

\[
\delta_{\mathrm{new}} \ge \left(1 - \frac{(2m+1)^{2}\,\delta}{3}\right)(2m+1)^{2}\,\delta .
\]

**Theorem 4 [amplitude estimation]**. \(\mathrm{Estimate}(A,c,p,k)\) uses expected \(\Theta\!\left(k\sqrt{(1+\log\log(1/p)) / \max(\varepsilon, p)}\right)\) evaluations of \(A\).

### 3.3 The state generation algorithm

Assume \(t_{i}=2^{i}\), \(i\in\{0,\ldots,m\}\). Algorithms \(A_{i}\) approximate

\[
|\psi_{i}^{\prime}\rangle = \frac{\alpha_{i,1}|1\rangle|\psi_{i,1}\rangle + \alpha_{i,2}|2\rangle|\psi_{i,2}\rangle}{\sqrt{|\alpha_{i,1}|^{2} + |\alpha_{i,2}|^{2}}}
\]

so that \(A_{i}\) outputs \(|\psi_{i}^{\prime\prime}\rangle = \sqrt{r_{i}}|\psi_{i}^{\prime}\rangle + \sqrt{1-r_{i}}|0\rangle|\phi_{i}\rangle\) with \(r_{i} \ge 1/(9m)\).

\(B_{i}\): for \(i=0\), run \(A\) one step. For \(i>0\), run \(A_{i-1}\), then \(A\) for time steps \(2^{i-1}\to 2^{i}\) on the outcome-register-\(2\) parts.

\(A_{i}\): if \(p > 1/(9m)\), \(A_{i}=B_{i}\); else \(A_{i}=\mathrm{Amplify}(B_{i}, k)\) for smallest \(k\) with \(1/(9m) \le (2k+1)^{2} p \le 1/m\).

**Algorithm 3 (\(A^{\prime}\)):** run Estimate for \(p_{0}\); for \(i=1,\ldots,m\), define \(A_{i},B_{i}\) from \(p_{i-1}\), and (if \(i<m\)) run Estimate for \(p_{i}\); finally amplify \(A_{m}\) to \(\ge 1/2\).

**Lemma 2 (recursion).**
\[
T_{i} \le \left(1 + \frac{1}{3m-1}\right)\sqrt{\frac{r_{i}}{r_{i}^{\prime}}}\, T_{i-1} + 2^{i-1}.
\]
By recursion, \(T_{m} = O\!\left( T_{\max}\log T_{\max} + \dfrac{T_{\mathrm{av}}}{\sqrt{p_{\mathrm{succ}}}}\log^{1.5} T_{\max}\right)\).

## 4 Faster HHL

Precision at branch \(i\) is \(2^{-i}\). The "good" amplitude in a stopped branch has magnitude \(O(1/(\kappa\widetilde{\lambda}_{i}))\). Applying VTAA to the doubling-schedule eigenvalue estimation, the worst-case dependence collapses from \(\kappa^{2}\) to \(\kappa\log^{3}\kappa\); Theorem 3 formalises the exact polylog exponents.

